37f1a672-cca3-49b4-9190-aeb5af956503	ECB SAFE survey: lending conditions for euro area firms tightened	The ECB's Survey on the Access to Finance of Enterprises reports that lending conditions for firms have tightened.	The European Central Bank published its latest Survey on the Access to Finance of Enterprises (SAFE). It indicates that lending conditions faced by firms tightened. The release headline gives no figures.\n\nTighter credit conditions could weigh on euro area business investment and hiring, which would likely dampen growth momentum. If that persists, it could add to the case for a more accommodative ECB stance. That could push euro area yields lower and weigh on the euro, with possible pressure on EURUSD.\n\nThe effects on global markets are likely modest. However, a softer euro could lend mild support to the US dollar index.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261026~bde7b6d11a.en.html)	2026-10-26 09:00:00+00	economy	{EURUSD,DXY}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261026~bde7b6d11a.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:05:11.048951+00
15f889be-18bc-46e1-8f26-e0a9b7547fcf	ECB's Frank Elderson: Taking action for nature – investing in economic resilience and prosperity	ECB official Frank Elderson delivered a speech arguing that action to protect nature is an investment in economic resilience and prosperity.	ECB official Frank Elderson gave a speech titled "Taking action for nature – investing in economic resilience and prosperity," published by the European Central Bank. The item provides no further detail on the speech's content.\n\nThe theme suggests a focus on nature-related and environmental risks to the economy and the financial system rather than near-term monetary policy. Any market impact is likely limited. Such remarks could reinforce supervisory attention to how banks manage nature-related risks, with possible longer-term implications for lending and investment flows. No direct signal on interest rates or the euro is evident from the information provided.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261026~d903d7ad85.en.html)	2026-10-26 05:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261026~d903d7ad85.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:05:23.276988+00
