cbcca502-f0b5-4ad6-92d5-6e17f6ebb56d	Category 2 Hurricane Isaias nears landfall, leading to production shut-ins	As of 4:00 a.m. central time on October 9, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration expects Hurricane Isaias to make landfall on the Gulf Coast late Friday or early Saturday, creating the potential for significant disruptions to energy infrastructure. Hurricane Isaias 	As of 4:00 a.m. central time on October 9, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration expects Hurricane Isaias to make landfall on the Gulf Coast late Friday or early Saturday, creating the potential for significant disruptions to energy infrastructure. Hurricane Isaias is the first hurricane of the 2026 Atlantic season and will be the largest storm system to make landfall in the region since Hurricane Helene in September 2024.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68284)	2026-10-29 19:59:34.214897+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68284	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747892+00
86884b91-3464-46e6-9059-9bf00d81dffc	Mixed outlook for energy expenditures this winter	We expect energy expenditures this winter to vary because of diverging trends in energy prices among fuels and regional variation in forecast temperatures. We expect that lower prices for natural gas and propane will drive decreases in expenditures this year for the half of U.S. 	We expect energy expenditures this winter to vary because of diverging trends in energy prices among fuels and regional variation in forecast temperatures. We expect that lower prices for natural gas and propane will drive decreases in expenditures this year for the half of U.S. households heating with those fuels. Higher prices for electricity and heating oil drive increased expenditures for households heating with those fuels in our forecast.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68264)	2026-10-07 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68264	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747896+00
f8206e8a-4b08-427e-945e-61911b4c8a2b	Crude oil prices and refinery margins generally increased throughout the third quarter	Petroleum markets in the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26) were characterized by increasing prices for crude oil and petroleum products amid persistent conflict in the Middle East.	Petroleum markets in the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26) were characterized by increasing prices for crude oil and petroleum products amid persistent conflict in the Middle East.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68245)	2026-10-05 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68245	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.7479+00
04cf9c25-c4d8-4784-9201-40c5b03b999c	U.S. exports of propane reached records in the first half of 2026	U.S. propane exports increased to 2 million barrels per day (b/d) in the first half of 2026, 11% more than during the same period in 2025. Increased U.S. propane production and changing trade flows, particularly increased exports to India and other countries in South Asia, drove 	U.S. propane exports increased to 2 million barrels per day (b/d) in the first half of 2026, 11% more than during the same period in 2025. Increased U.S. propane production and changing trade flows, particularly increased exports to India and other countries in South Asia, drove propane exports to a record high of 2.1 million (b/d) in April 2026. Since then, total propane exports, including exports to India, have continued at levels well above the five-year average.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68244)	2026-10-01 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68244	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747914+00
9b9347db-5b91-44d8-94c4-6e4924d56b99	U.S. electricity use for electric vehicles increasing at a slower pace in 2026	We estimate light-duty electric vehicles in the United States consumed 8% more electricity during the first six months of 2026 than in the last half of 2025 (2H25) in our Monthly Energy Review, down from the 13-24% growth seen in six-month periods in recent years. The slowing ele	We estimate light-duty electric vehicles in the United States consumed 8% more electricity during the first six months of 2026 than in the last half of 2025 (2H25) in our Monthly Energy Review, down from the 13-24% growth seen in six-month periods in recent years. The slowing electric vehicle electricity consumption growth followed a decline in sales after U.S. federal tax credits expired in September 2025. Overall, U.S. light-duty electric vehicle electricity use has more than doubled since 1H2\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68224)	2026-09-30 14:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST10}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68224	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747919+00
44c9c06b-199d-43d8-b230-af3b9f477b95	Henry Hub natural gas prices this summer were 6% lower than last summer	The Henry Hub natural gas spot price averaged $2.93 per million British thermal units from June through August, 6% less than the same period last year. Prices were lower this summer despite exceptionally hot weather that increased electricity demand for air conditioning. Natural 	The Henry Hub natural gas spot price averaged $2.93 per million British thermal units from June through August, 6% less than the same period last year. Prices were lower this summer despite exceptionally hot weather that increased electricity demand for air conditioning. Natural gas-fired power plants often help meet higher demand during such periods.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68204)	2026-09-25 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68204	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747926+00
bde0e408-0e37-41b6-bfa5-dc3f5aa3bb2c	Public companies produce most U.S. crude oil and natural gas	Publicly traded companies represent a tiny share of the total number of companies producing crude oil and natural gas in the United States, but they make up a large share of U.S. production. In 2025, publicly traded companies accounted for just 2% of about 12,000 oil and natural 	Publicly traded companies represent a tiny share of the total number of companies producing crude oil and natural gas in the United States, but they make up a large share of U.S. production. In 2025, publicly traded companies accounted for just 2% of about 12,000 oil and natural gas producers but produced 68% of the crude oil and natural gas in the Lower 48 states.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68184)	2026-09-22 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68184	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747929+00
663bf179-0acb-4280-8c51-eb971ead032a	What goes into diesel prices?	The price of distillate fuel oil, often sold as diesel, is driven by the price of crude oil, retail margins, distribution costs, taxes, and crack spreads, the indicator we use for refining margins. Tight global supplies of distillate fuel oil and elevated crude oil prices have dr	The price of distillate fuel oil, often sold as diesel, is driven by the price of crude oil, retail margins, distribution costs, taxes, and crack spreads, the indicator we use for refining margins. Tight global supplies of distillate fuel oil and elevated crude oil prices have driven prices higher in recent months.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68164)	2026-09-18 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68164	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747934+00
